Note: SFZ Orchestral Sampler — opt-in via `use "@sfz"`

Note: Pull in std so the (dict ...) constructor + the
Note: shared (loadSfz Symbol) / (loadSfz String) forward-decls are visible
Note: by the time the trailing (__enableSfzModule ...) call runs. Composers
Note: who `use "@sfz"` therefore get @std transitively (matching the
Note: existing composition.flow → @audio precedent).

module sfz

use "@std"

Note: ===== Internal C# Function Declarations =====

Note: Marker builtin called once at module load (last expression of this file).
Note: Flips ExecutionContext.SfzEnabled = true and copies the 20-entry GM dict
Note: into ExecutionContext.SfzInstruments. Composers should not call this
Note: directly — the trailing (__enableSfzModule __sfzInstruments) line below
Note: handles it for them when the module is imported.
internal proc __enableSfzModule (Dict<Symbol, String>: instruments)

Note: The loadSfz(Symbol) + loadSfz(String) forward-decls live in std.flow
Note: so the function is always visible at parse-resolution time. The SfzEnabled
Note: runtime gate handles the "called without `use \"@sfz\"`" path
Note: — composer gets a "loadSfz requires `use \"@sfz\"`" error, not the generic
Note: "Function not found".

Note: ===== 20-entry GM Orchestral Instrument → VSCO-CE Relative-Path Dict =====
Note: Single source of truth — composers can `(get __sfzInstruments #violin)`
Note: from their own Flow code to inspect/extend. Edits to relative paths
Note: land here without a C# rebuild.
Note:
Note: 16 of 20 rows are verified against VSCO-CE 1.1.0.
Note: 4 rows are TBD because VSCO Community Edition does not bundle a patch
Note: for that GM symbol — the dict ships a known-missing placeholder path
Note: so `(loadSfz #choir)` errors with a clear "patch not found" message
Note: pointing the composer at the absolute-path overload.
Note:
Note: The `#drums` symbol drives `SfzData.IsPercussion = true`
Note: at SfzBuiltins load time, which gates the SfzRenderer's #auto
Note: pitch-shift route. Composers who want
Note: percussion routing via the absolute-path `(loadSfz "...")` overload
Note: opt out by construction.

Dict<Symbol, String> __sfzInstruments = (dict
    Note: ----- Strings (4 verified) -----
    #violin       "SViolinVib.sfz"
    #viola        "ViolaEnsSusVib.sfz"
    #cello        "CelloEnsSusVib.sfz"
    #contrabass   "ContrabassSusVB.sfz"
    Note: ----- Woodwinds (4 verified) -----
    #flute        "FluteSusVib.sfz"
    #oboe         "OboeSusVib.sfz"
    #clarinet     "ClarinetSus.sfz"
    #bassoon      "BassoonSus.sfz"
    Note: ----- Brass (4 verified) -----
    #trumpet      "TrumpetSus.sfz"
    #horn         "FHornSus.sfz"
    #trombone     "TromboneSus.sfz"
    #tuba         "TubaSus.sfz"
    Note: ----- Keys + Plucked + Percussion (4 verified) -----
    #piano        "UprightPiano.sfz"
    #harp         "Harp.sfz"
    #timpani      "Timpani.sfz"
    #drums        "GM-StylePerc.sfz"
    Note: ----- 4 TBD rows (not bundled with VSCO-CE 1.1.0) -----
    Note: composer downloads externally; absolute-path overload is the workaround
    #choir        "_TBD_choir_not_in_vsco-ce.sfz"
    #guitar       "_TBD_guitar_not_in_vsco-ce.sfz"
    #harpsichord  "_TBD_harpsichord_not_in_vsco-ce.sfz"
    #celeste      "_TBD_celeste_not_in_vsco-ce.sfz"
)

Note: ===== Side-effecting init marker =====
Note: Flips ExecutionContext.SfzEnabled = true and registers the dict for
Note: loadSfz #symbol lookups. Without this line, the C# builtins remain
Note: registered but always raise "loadSfz requires `use \"@sfz\"`".
(__enableSfzModule __sfzInstruments)
